maya: Quick help wanted — we keep getting messy ESM/CJS import issues in the JS SDK around `fetch`. Node 18+ has global `fetch`, but we still bundle `undici` for older Node and some customers. Need a concise recommended approach that covers: ESM/CJS interop, edge runtime compatibility (Cloudflare/Vercel/Deno), and TypeScript types. Anyone built a short pattern we can paste into the README?

sam: TL;DR: prefer global `fetch` when present, lazy-load `undici` as a Node fallback, and ship both ESM + CJS builds with conditional exports. Super small runtime shim example: 
```js
// src/getFetch.ts
export async function getFetch() {
  if (globalThis.fetch) return globalThis.fetch;
  // lazy import so CJS/older Node doesn't crash on import time
  const { fetch } = await import('undici');
  return fetch;
}
```
This keeps edge runtimes (which have global `fetch`) clean and avoids bundling `undici` into edge deployments.

olivia: Packaging notes: if we publish as ESM-only (`"type": "module"`) some CJS consumers choke. Use conditional exports to provide both builds and avoid leading consumers to weird require/import errors. Example `package.json` fragment: 
```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
  }
},
"type": "module"
```
Also produce both bundles with tsup/rollup. Keep the runtime shim in `dist/` as both `.mjs` and `.cjs`.

kevin: Typing pain point: TS projects that target Node-only often don't include the DOM lib so `RequestInit`, `Response`, etc. are missing. Options:
- Document that consumers targeting Node should enable `"lib": ["ES2020", "DOM"]` or use `@types/node` that includes fetch types.
- Ship a tiny `types/fetch.d.ts` in the package that re-exports minimal types so the SDK's public API types compile for downstream TS without forcing DOM lib. Example: 
```ts
// types/fetch.d.ts
export type FetchFn = (input: RequestInfo, init?: RequestInit) => Promise<Response>
declare const _default: FetchFn
export default _default
```
This is minimal and avoids surprising compiler errors. If you must, suggest `skipLibCheck` as a last resort for downstream consumers.

sam: Edge runtimes: Cloudflare Workers, Vercel Edge, Bun, Deno all provide `fetch`. So make sure your build targets don't accidentally polyfill or bundle `undici` for edge targets. Use conditional exports + proper `browser`/`module` fields or rely on tooling (esbuild/rollup) to prefer `module`/`browser` fields.

olivia: Also watch for CJS consumers doing `const { getFetch } = require('my-sdk')` — if the ESM build uses top-level `await` or dynamic `import()` semantics, ensure the CJS build provides a sync-compatible surface or at least a clear error message. Our pattern: keep `getFetch()` async but exported from both builds; CJS consumers must await it. Add an explicit note in README.

maya: Any short runnable runtime-detection snippet we can paste? I want something copy-paste friendly for examples.

sam: Yup — a compact pattern: 
```js
// safeFetch.js (consumer-friendly)
export async function safeFetch(...args) {
  const f = globalThis.fetch ?? (await import('undici')).fetch;
  return f(...args);
}
```
Note: dynamic import keeps `undici` out of edge bundles and avoids import-time errors in CJS/Early Node.

